1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,350
On September sixteenth, 1944,

2
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:06,371
a group of Japanese prisoners of war sat

3
00:00:06,391 --> 00:00:09,393
down to lunch on an island in San

4
00:00:09,413 --> 00:00:10,148
Francisco Bay.

5
00:00:10,168 --> 00:00:13,170
Every one of them knew what capture was

6
00:00:13,190 --> 00:00:14,303
supposed to mean.

7
00:00:14,323 --> 00:00:16,191
Torture first, and then death.

8
00:00:16,211 --> 00:00:18,079
Lunch was sukiyaki, cabbage salad,

9
00:00:18,099 --> 00:00:19,610
rice, and caramel pudding.

10
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:22,447
Nobody had beaten them.

11
00:00:22,467 --> 00:00:24,723
The prisoners played cards and Mah-Jong,

12
00:00:24,743 --> 00:00:26,619
listened to the camp gramophone,

13
00:00:26,639 --> 00:00:28,136
and whispered among themselves,

14
00:00:28,156 --> 00:00:30,791
trying to work out what their captors

15
00:00:30,811 --> 00:00:31,611
really wanted.

16
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:34,495
They were right to wonder.

17
00:00:34,515 --> 00:00:36,464
The food was not an accident.

18
00:00:36,484 --> 00:00:39,089
It was the first step of a plan.

19
00:00:39,109 --> 00:00:41,713
Within days, many of these men would be

20
00:00:41,733 --> 00:00:44,338
driven to a former luxury hotel in the

21
00:00:44,358 --> 00:00:46,963
hills east of the bay, where the walls

22
00:00:46,983 --> 00:00:48,295
were wired for sound.

23
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,492
These men were rare.

24
00:00:51,512 --> 00:00:53,759
The Japanese military field code told

25
00:00:53,779 --> 00:00:56,405
every soldier that rather than live and

26
00:00:56,425 --> 00:00:58,294
bear the shame of imprisonment,

27
00:00:58,314 --> 00:00:59,428
he should die.

28
00:00:59,448 --> 00:01:02,452
Soldiers were told to save the last round

29
00:01:02,472 --> 00:01:03,208
for themselves.

30
00:01:03,228 --> 00:01:06,231
From the first shots of the Pacific war

31
00:01:06,251 --> 00:01:08,121
through Midway, Guadalcanal and Tarawa,

32
00:01:08,141 --> 00:01:11,144
the Allies took only six hundred and four

33
00:01:11,164 --> 00:01:11,964
Japanese prisoners.

34
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:15,524
Some of that was the code.

35
00:01:15,544 --> 00:01:17,768
Some of it was the Americans.

36
00:01:17,788 --> 00:01:20,011
The army's own history admitted that

37
00:01:20,031 --> 00:01:22,255
American troops, fearful of the enemy's

38
00:01:22,275 --> 00:01:24,125
reputation for treachery, were reluctant

39
00:01:24,145 --> 00:01:25,995
to take prisoners at all.

40
00:01:26,015 --> 00:01:27,491
One confidential intelligence memo

41
00:01:27,511 --> 00:01:30,483
conceded that it had taken the promise of

42
00:01:30,503 --> 00:01:33,474
three days' leave and some ice cream to

43
00:01:33,494 --> 00:01:36,466
get soldiers to bring in the first live

44
00:01:36,486 --> 00:01:37,286
prisoner.

45
00:01:38,175 --> 00:01:40,695
On Guadalcanal, in the first six weeks of

46
00:01:40,715 --> 00:01:44,094
1943, the American Fourteenth Corps took

47
00:01:44,114 --> 00:01:45,258
eighty-four Japanese prisoners.

48
00:01:45,278 --> 00:01:47,973
Thirty-three of them were too sick or

49
00:01:47,993 --> 00:01:49,137
wounded to walk.

50
00:01:49,157 --> 00:01:51,852
To army intelligence, a prisoner who could

51
00:01:51,872 --> 00:01:54,975
talk was rare, and worth a great deal.

52
00:01:56,225 --> 00:01:59,014
So the Americans kept the ones they

53
00:01:59,034 --> 00:01:59,416
wanted.

54
00:01:59,436 --> 00:02:01,824
Under an agreement with Australia in

55
00:02:01,844 --> 00:02:04,858
September 1942, most Japanese captives

56
00:02:04,878 --> 00:02:06,884
were handed over to the Australians.

57
00:02:06,904 --> 00:02:09,586
The men shipped to the United States were

58
00:02:09,606 --> 00:02:11,274
chosen largely for one reason:

59
00:02:11,294 --> 00:02:12,645
what they might know.

60
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:16,485
They came ashore at Angel Island,

61
00:02:16,505 --> 00:02:19,425
a small, steep island in San Francisco Bay

62
00:02:19,445 --> 00:02:21,998
that had been a quarantine station for

63
00:02:22,018 --> 00:02:23,468
immigrants before the war.

64
00:02:23,488 --> 00:02:25,672
They were deloused, given serial numbers,

65
00:02:25,692 --> 00:02:27,877
and examined for malaria, skin disease,

66
00:02:27,898 --> 00:02:29,000
worms and wounds.

67
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:32,037
Then came a postal card.

68
00:02:32,057 --> 00:02:34,769
Every prisoner could send one to tell his

69
00:02:34,789 --> 00:02:37,843
family he was alive, and his name could be

70
00:02:37,863 --> 00:02:39,550
passed to the Red Cross.

71
00:02:39,570 --> 00:02:42,282
Nearly all of them asked for the opposite.

72
00:02:42,302 --> 00:02:44,672
They asked that their families not be

73
00:02:44,692 --> 00:02:45,014
told.

74
00:02:45,034 --> 00:02:48,087
Better to be thought dead than known to be

75
00:02:48,107 --> 00:02:48,907
a prisoner.

76
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:53,405
They expected to be tortured and killed,

77
00:02:53,425 --> 00:02:55,458
and from where they stood,

78
00:02:55,478 --> 00:02:57,101
the fear made sense.

79
00:02:57,121 --> 00:02:58,743
They despised their captors,

80
00:02:58,763 --> 00:03:02,029
and they knew they were hated in return.

81
00:03:02,049 --> 00:03:05,314
What they got instead was the menu of

82
00:03:05,334 --> 00:03:06,135
September sixteenth.

83
00:03:06,155 --> 00:03:08,599
Sausages, rice and apples for breakfast.

84
00:03:08,619 --> 00:03:11,063
Sukiyaki and caramel pudding for lunch.

85
00:03:11,083 --> 00:03:13,117
Spaghetti and hash, baked tomatoes,

86
00:03:13,137 --> 00:03:15,190
cakes and cocoa for dinner.

87
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,265
The meals were written down by Red Cross

88
00:03:19,285 --> 00:03:21,353
and State Department inspectors that day.

89
00:03:21,373 --> 00:03:23,094
The prisoners, the record shows,

90
00:03:23,114 --> 00:03:25,878
were better fed in captivity than they had

91
00:03:25,898 --> 00:03:27,619
been in their own army.

92
00:03:27,639 --> 00:03:29,707
But for a newly captured man,

93
00:03:29,727 --> 00:03:32,492
the first meal in America was hard to

94
00:03:32,512 --> 00:03:33,312
explain.

95
00:03:34,625 --> 00:03:37,000
The War Department had its reasons.

96
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:39,395
These prisoners had been brought to

97
00:03:39,415 --> 00:03:41,790
America to be questioned, and frightened

98
00:03:41,810 --> 00:03:43,387
men made poor sources.

99
00:03:43,407 --> 00:03:45,782
Army intelligence had already learned that

100
00:03:45,802 --> 00:03:47,778
Japanese prisoners passed through three

101
00:03:47,798 --> 00:03:48,576
distinct phases.

102
00:03:48,596 --> 00:03:51,770
Get the timing right, and a man who

103
00:03:51,790 --> 00:03:54,185
expected to die would talk freely.

104
00:03:55,525 --> 00:03:57,397
In the first forty-eight hours,

105
00:03:57,417 --> 00:03:59,288
a prisoner was nearly useless.

106
00:03:59,308 --> 00:04:02,314
He was sure he was about to die,

107
00:04:02,334 --> 00:04:05,341
and he either said nothing or said things

108
00:04:05,361 --> 00:04:07,232
that could not be trusted.

109
00:04:07,252 --> 00:04:09,124
After several days, something changed.

110
00:04:09,144 --> 00:04:12,150
He realized nobody was going to hurt him.

111
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:13,664
Fear turned into gratitude,

112
00:04:13,684 --> 00:04:16,690
and gratitude into a wish to repay his

113
00:04:16,710 --> 00:04:17,845
captors by talking.

114
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:22,141
That window lasted ten days to two weeks.

115
00:04:22,161 --> 00:04:24,023
Then came the third phase.

116
00:04:24,043 --> 00:04:27,410
The prisoner got used to the food and the

117
00:04:27,430 --> 00:04:29,668
kindness, grew annoyed at the questions,

118
00:04:29,688 --> 00:04:31,550
and withdrew into a shell.

119
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:34,561
After that, he was no longer a reliable

120
00:04:34,581 --> 00:04:34,937
source.

121
00:04:34,957 --> 00:04:38,325
The sukiyaki was not the end of the plan.

122
00:04:38,345 --> 00:04:39,850
It started the clock.

123
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:44,574
The clock ran at a place called Byron Hot

124
00:04:44,594 --> 00:04:44,918
Springs.

125
00:04:44,938 --> 00:04:48,280
A resort had opened there in 1889,

126
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:51,159
around mineral springs in the hills of

127
00:04:51,179 --> 00:04:52,393
Contra Costa County.

128
00:04:52,413 --> 00:04:56,840
A four-story brick hotel opened in 1914.

129
00:04:56,860 --> 00:04:58,940
In the nineteen-twenties it drew wealthy

130
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:01,390
guests and celebrities to its mud baths

131
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:02,460
and mineral soaks.

132
00:05:03,625 --> 00:05:05,725
The resort closed in 1938,

133
00:05:05,745 --> 00:05:08,111
after a series of lawsuits.

134
00:05:08,131 --> 00:05:12,321
In May 1942, the War Department chose it

135
00:05:12,341 --> 00:05:14,892
for one of only two secret interrogation

136
00:05:14,912 --> 00:05:16,729
centers in the United States.

137
00:05:16,749 --> 00:05:19,300
The other was Fort Hunt, near Washington.

138
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:22,605
On paper, it was known only by an address

139
00:05:22,625 --> 00:05:24,074
that gave nothing away:

140
00:05:24,094 --> 00:05:25,910
Post Office Box six fifty-one,

141
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:26,730
Tracy, California.

142
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:30,140
The construction plans were labeled

143
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:30,944
Officers' School.

144
00:05:30,964 --> 00:05:34,161
As one historian put it, the workers must

145
00:05:34,181 --> 00:05:36,171
have wondered why officers needed

146
00:05:36,191 --> 00:05:38,182
eight-foot fences, barred windows and

147
00:05:38,202 --> 00:05:38,986
heavy gates.

148
00:05:39,006 --> 00:05:41,398
Microphones were hidden in the light

149
00:05:41,418 --> 00:05:41,800
fixtures.

150
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:45,017
Wires ran through hollow walls and down an

151
00:05:45,037 --> 00:05:47,429
elevator shaft to a transcription room,

152
00:05:47,449 --> 00:05:49,440
where the prisoners' conversations were

153
00:05:49,460 --> 00:05:51,470
listened to and written down.

154
00:05:52,750 --> 00:05:55,215
The camp never held more than fifty-one

155
00:05:55,235 --> 00:05:56,635
prisoners at a time.

156
00:05:56,655 --> 00:05:59,830
Some vents let a man standing on a toilet

157
00:05:59,850 --> 00:06:02,315
see into the next room, which researchers

158
00:06:02,335 --> 00:06:04,800
believe was meant to get prisoners talking

159
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:05,865
to each other.

160
00:06:05,885 --> 00:06:07,995
Even the trees outside carried listening

161
00:06:08,015 --> 00:06:08,815
devices.

162
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:11,791
There were forty-five interrogators,

163
00:06:11,811 --> 00:06:13,804
and many were Japanese Americans,

164
00:06:13,824 --> 00:06:15,818
trained at the army's Military

165
00:06:15,838 --> 00:06:17,831
Intelligence Service Language School in

166
00:06:17,851 --> 00:06:18,234
Minnesota.

167
00:06:18,254 --> 00:06:20,650
Each interrogator was given one prisoner,

168
00:06:20,670 --> 00:06:23,469
and stayed with him until there was

169
00:06:23,489 --> 00:06:25,100
nothing left to learn.

170
00:06:26,375 --> 00:06:28,552
On the surface, the sessions were

171
00:06:28,572 --> 00:06:28,918
informal:

172
00:06:28,938 --> 00:06:30,748
forty-five minutes to an hour,

173
00:06:30,768 --> 00:06:34,409
two or three times a day, for a week or

174
00:06:34,429 --> 00:06:34,775
more.

175
00:06:34,795 --> 00:06:36,605
Underneath, every question was planned.

176
00:06:36,625 --> 00:06:38,436
A long checklist covered weapons,

177
00:06:38,456 --> 00:06:39,900
fuel, rations, morale, rumors,

178
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:42,463
commanders, and what a man expected after

179
00:06:42,483 --> 00:06:43,283
the war.

180
00:06:44,475 --> 00:06:47,304
Every four days or so, the findings went

181
00:06:47,324 --> 00:06:48,728
out in short reports.

182
00:06:48,748 --> 00:06:51,221
They ranged from the falling quality of

183
00:06:51,241 --> 00:06:53,714
Japanese clothing to the number of boiler

184
00:06:53,734 --> 00:06:55,851
rooms in the aircraft carrier Hiryu.

185
00:06:55,871 --> 00:06:59,048
The camp's report for 1944 counted one

186
00:06:59,068 --> 00:07:00,833
thousand and seventy-seven Japanese

187
00:07:00,853 --> 00:07:03,064
prisoners questioned, from thirteen in

188
00:07:03,084 --> 00:07:06,189
January to one hundred and seventy-four in

189
00:07:06,209 --> 00:07:07,009
August.

190
00:07:08,450 --> 00:07:10,802
What made them talk was rarely force.

191
00:07:10,822 --> 00:07:13,174
Army intelligence found that no threat of

192
00:07:13,194 --> 00:07:15,208
violence or solitary confinement worked as

193
00:07:15,228 --> 00:07:16,902
well as a simpler one:

194
00:07:16,922 --> 00:07:19,613
the threat to send a prisoner's name to

195
00:07:19,633 --> 00:07:20,969
his family in Japan.

196
00:07:20,989 --> 00:07:23,680
The men who had asked that their families

197
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:26,750
never be told now had the most to lose.

198
00:07:28,075 --> 00:07:29,984
Another lesson surprised the Americans.

199
00:07:30,004 --> 00:07:32,299
German prisoners had to be questioned

200
00:07:32,319 --> 00:07:34,229
alone, before older prisoners could

201
00:07:34,249 --> 00:07:35,001
pressure them.

202
00:07:35,021 --> 00:07:37,702
With the Japanese, it worked the other

203
00:07:37,722 --> 00:07:38,087
way.

204
00:07:38,107 --> 00:07:40,788
Older prisoners told new arrivals to tell

205
00:07:40,808 --> 00:07:43,489
everything, so that the whole group would

206
00:07:43,509 --> 00:07:45,419
not be blamed for lying.

207
00:07:45,439 --> 00:07:47,734
Some came back the next day,

208
00:07:47,754 --> 00:07:50,455
on their own, to correct their answers.

209
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:54,594
None of this was strictly legal.

210
00:07:54,614 --> 00:07:57,008
The secret centers, the historian Arnold

211
00:07:57,028 --> 00:07:59,824
Krammer concluded, bent or broke about a

212
00:07:59,844 --> 00:08:02,640
dozen articles of the Geneva Convention of

213
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:04,580
1929.

214
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:07,214
Japan had never ratified that convention.

215
00:08:07,234 --> 00:08:09,848
The War Department decided the information

216
00:08:09,868 --> 00:08:13,360
was worth the risk, and reasoned that the

217
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:15,555
treatment of American prisoners in

218
00:08:15,575 --> 00:08:18,628
Japanese hands could hardly be made worse.

219
00:08:18,648 --> 00:08:20,384
The comfort was real.

220
00:08:20,404 --> 00:08:22,160
So was its purpose.

221
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:25,349
And the questions had an end.

222
00:08:25,369 --> 00:08:27,842
Once a prisoner had nothing more to give,

223
00:08:27,862 --> 00:08:30,958
he was put in a group and shipped out to

224
00:08:30,978 --> 00:08:31,893
an ordinary camp:

225
00:08:31,913 --> 00:08:33,762
McCoy in Wisconsin, Clarinda in Iowa,

226
00:08:33,782 --> 00:08:35,340
or back to Angel Island.

227
00:08:37,050 --> 00:08:40,127
For some, the change went far deeper than

228
00:08:40,147 --> 00:08:40,515
talking.

229
00:08:40,535 --> 00:08:43,612
They had been taught that their lives as

230
00:08:43,632 --> 00:08:45,161
Japanese ended at capture.

231
00:08:45,181 --> 00:08:48,258
When their requests to be allowed to kill

232
00:08:48,278 --> 00:08:50,581
themselves were refused, many started over

233
00:08:50,601 --> 00:08:51,743
as model prisoners.

234
00:08:51,763 --> 00:08:55,111
In 1946, the anthropologist Ruth Benedict

235
00:08:55,131 --> 00:08:57,573
described old army hands who located

236
00:08:57,593 --> 00:08:59,625
ammunition dumps, explained the positions

237
00:08:59,645 --> 00:09:01,676
of Japanese forces, wrote American

238
00:09:01,696 --> 00:09:04,138
propaganda, and flew with American bombing

239
00:09:04,158 --> 00:09:07,030
pilots to guide them to military targets.

240
00:09:08,350 --> 00:09:10,889
One group of prisoners went further still.

241
00:09:10,909 --> 00:09:13,448
They announced that they had been badly

242
00:09:13,468 --> 00:09:16,007
misled by the Emperor and the military

243
00:09:16,027 --> 00:09:18,931
clique, and asked to fight their way back

244
00:09:18,951 --> 00:09:20,759
to Japan alongside Allied soldiers.

245
00:09:20,779 --> 00:09:22,221
Benedict's explanation was simple.

246
00:09:22,241 --> 00:09:24,780
Having put everything into one way of

247
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:27,704
living and failed at it, they took up

248
00:09:27,724 --> 00:09:28,524
another.

249
00:09:29,425 --> 00:09:32,990
In September 1945, the army ordered the

250
00:09:33,010 --> 00:09:35,203
camp closed and took its equipment away.

251
00:09:35,223 --> 00:09:38,048
One of the two centers was not revealed to

252
00:09:38,068 --> 00:09:42,245
the public until the summer of 1947.

253
00:09:42,265 --> 00:09:44,386
That same year, the Greek Orthodox Church

254
00:09:44,406 --> 00:09:46,527
bought Byron Hot Springs for one hundred

255
00:09:46,547 --> 00:09:48,668
and five thousand dollars and turned it

256
00:09:48,688 --> 00:09:49,605
into a monastery.

257
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,196
If you know of a prisoner or an

258
00:09:53,216 --> 00:09:54,858
interrogator who passed through Byron Hot

259
00:09:54,878 --> 00:09:56,540
Springs, put it in the comments.

260
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:01,446
The brick hotel still stands in the hills,

261
00:10:01,466 --> 00:10:01,841
abandoned.

262
00:10:01,861 --> 00:10:04,216
Nothing about it says what happened

263
00:10:04,236 --> 00:10:04,611
inside.

264
00:10:04,631 --> 00:10:06,986
The prisoners who arrived there had

265
00:10:07,006 --> 00:10:08,569
prepared themselves for pain.

266
00:10:08,589 --> 00:10:11,339
What the Americans used instead began with

267
00:10:11,359 --> 00:10:14,109
a plate of sukiyaki, and someone willing

268
00:10:14,129 --> 00:10:14,920
to listen.

